  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Institutional strengthening of Hutukara Associa&#xe7;&#xe3;o Yanomami (HAY)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Hutukara Yanomami Association was created in 2004 to represent the Yanomami people externally and is engaged directly in policy, advocacy and self-protection initiatives. HAY is a representative indigenous grassroots organisation which undertakes long distance communications and regional meetings with indigenous communities to identify the key threats and issues that they face, and then uses this information to lobby key governmental bodies, such as FUNAI, DSEI-Y and SESAI to protect indigenous rights. The project aims to strength Hutukara&apos;s leaders&apos; ability to advocate on behalf of the Yanomami and to improve living conditions in Yanomami indigenous territory (TIY).

HAY works with the approximately 17,000 Yanomami living in TIY that are relatively isolated, with little access to the outside world. Their communities were invaded by over 40,000 gold miners in the 1980s, and 20% of the Yanomami people died over the course of 7 years due to disease and violence. The Yanomami and Ye&apos;kuana people were officially granted rights to the TIY in 1982, however land invasions continue, such as illegal gold-miners and farmers, and communities are increasingly feeling the impacts of climate change, which is affecting their health, food security and environment. Other key challenges that the communities are facing include inadequate health service provision in TIY, lack of access to quality bi-lingual education and the need for indigenous cultural preservation. 

The aim of this project is to &#x2018;Strengthen the presence of HAY in the grassroots communities by working directly with the people that inhabit the Yanomami Indigenous Territory (TIY)&#x2019;. They intend to do this through three outcomes-
1.	20 communities within the TIY are communicating and discussing with HAY, which will increase the participation of traditional leaders, young people and women,
2.             HAY develops the land management plan for TIY
3.             HAY&#x2019;s administrative and financial management systems are improved.</narrative>
  </description>
